air  i demand the right to go and see him in  person about the thing that's not really  that bad and also those who quite  rightly complain that you know they  haven't been properly diagnosed because  they haven't been able to get in to see  their gp and here we can see some of the  the reason for that which is that you  know the paper there's reminding us in  2015 the government promised to hire 5  000 more gps over the next five years so  we should have them by now right um only  actually the numbers they haven't got  those five thousand and the numbers  dropped by four further thirteen hundred  so uh the gp numbers are five percent  below what they should be  uh and like anybody in any walk of life  if you're a tv presenter or journalist  or you're stacking yourself whatever it  might be if there are fewer of you in  the office doing the job then the the  job you do have to do you have to work  harder do it um faster do it for the  same money and  don't do quite such a good job as you  would do otherwise which is why cutbacks  everywhere are usually a terrible idea  and that's what's happened here gps are  saying here that they just don't feel  now as safe as they should be and  if the gps don't feel safe then the  patients aren't going to feel any safer  either are they  the articles pointing to uh professor  martin marshall chair of the royal  college of gps and he's defending the  growing use of remote consultations  during the pandemic anita but we have  heard previously i think in the last  couple of days from coroners saying that  the deaths that they've seen are  potentially due to the fact that  patients couldn't see their gps for  face-to-face consultations in  time yes it feels as though some of the  quality and some of the nuance that  perhaps you might be able to get via a  face-to-face consultation is being lost  by a remote consultations look the  entire system has to have a degree of  flexibility because obviously because of  i think the thing that susie's probably  missed from that account is the growing  demand that's being placed on gps both  because of general population growth  backlog and all the other things that  are not really affected and of the  result of any government policy  decisions and so you need to get a  balance between that increased  efficiency that you might see  remote consultations and obviously the  need for face-to-face contact to  continue  let's move on to the express um and this  is a story that we've all been uh too  aware of uh the fact that it's being  predicted that things are running out  the shelves are empty um what is the the  remedy to preventing our shells being  empty uh just before christmas  susie  well according to the daily express  which campaign loudly for brexit this uh  hasn't had the word brexit anywhere on  the front page  uh it's blamed the shortage of drivers  on the government not doing enough and  what course the government hasn't done  uh is allow visas for foreign drivers to  come in and try and fill this shortage  that we've got at the very least in the  short term but more interestingly is if  you kind of look elsewhere and the  express isn't really mentioning it here  is that the chairman of the food and  drink federation said today this isn't  just a driver shortage issue this is a  problem of how our supply chains have  been built over time you know the  british economy was was  built in such a way that was called just  in time alright so whenever you went to  the shop you had anything you wanted in  that shop pretty much and they were  always delivered at the last minute and  being in eu enabled that to happen  because of the changes that have been  brought about the ceo of the food and  drink federation  right says that that's never going to  happen again now he does say it's a  first world problem for example last  week uh i think people in the east of  england weren't able to have deliveries  of bottled water which of course you can  get out of the tap it's not the end of  the world not to have deliveries a  bottled water but it does mean that when  you go to the shops he says forever from  now on not just at christmas not just  because of a bit of a brexit hiccup but  forever  our supply chains have been radically  and fundamentally altered in the long  term and there will be shortages on  shelves he says long past christmas and  even if the government does actually  provide some uh  some new licenses and testing here for  hgv drivers which is what the store is  talking about of course that puts up  prices for what you get on the shelves  at christmas if they can get it there at  all so then you're looking at inflation  you're looking at your money not going  as far as it should do and a lot of  people's christmas is going to be  significantly tighter and worse than it  could otherwise be anita stories like  this are going to lead to people panic  buying aren't they i mean we've seen it  before in the early stages of the the  pandemic with uh people stocking up  unnecessarily on on toilet rolls but  stories like this now  really could lead to to a panic  i'm not quite so doom and gloom about  this obviously the you know when it  comes to christmas lots of people do  tend to buy a little more and that puts  pressure on the chain in general look i  i think susie makes an interesting point  about the ways in which the way that we  shop has been engineered over the past  sort of two decades to ensure that we  keep prices low and margins incredibly  low and that has made it very  challenging for the model around  delivery of food in particular to change  um but i think part of the story that's  missing probably from that account is  really about the pay and conditions of  some of these drivers that deliver um  the products that we rely on on in that  just just-in-time way and i think what  we're really seeing here is some of that  coming to a head where the paying  conditions as well as a pandemic have  forced people into different professions  and there needs to be a kind of  consideration of what we can do in terms  of that pay and conditions the  fundamental issue to address um what has  led to a fall in supply of these drivers  and i think that fundamentally you know  we can do what we can do around ensuring  that people can have those tests but  really it's a pain the conditions are  going to make a difference to getting  those lorry driver numbers up to ensure  that we have something that looks much  more like what we're used to outside of  covert time susie to the times and uh  contrary to what dominic cummings would  have us believe it seems the prime  minister  would like to stay in the job a little  bit longer dominic cummings was  suggesting uh prior to to this that uh  he was actually sick of it and couldn't  take the the difficulty of the task but  what do we believe  well  um it's not just dominic cummings  there's been a number of sort of uh  leaks and so on from within daniel  street that have sort of said boris  johnson liking the job of being prime  ministers not being as much fun as he  thought it was going to be and likening  it to sort of strapping a jumbo jet onto  your back every morning and trying to  drag it along the runway um and i think  anybody who actually looks at the man  can see that he he doesn't look exactly  in fine fettle and doesn't like he's  enjoying it that much and also isn't  having much fun because all the things  he would want to do that would be fun  mad bridges and air reports and stuff  like this just aren't happening and he's  having lots of arguments and problems  however  what this story is about is saying boris  johnson saying i want to be in power for  11 years and there's a minister who has  briefed uh the times about this and he  wants to go on longer than thatcher  which some people are going to be crying  blood over that idea but  really interestingly here um let's do  everything with boris johnson you don't  listen to what the man says but you have  to look at what the man actually does  because they're usually completely  opposite things and right next to this  story on the front page there's one  about how major planning reforms have  had to be utterly scrapped because he is  going to lose seats right across the  south if he carries on with the idea of  um allowing some of these planning  reforms which would stop people  objecting to new housing and so on and  so forth so that's big idea number one  straight out the window and if you turn  the page over this page four there's a  huge piece about how boris johnson's  gonna have to decide whether or not to  scrap the eastern leg of hs2 it was  already brief that that was going to be  scrapped he's going to make a decision  shortly but again it's another big  project another big scheme that he's not  able to push through doesn't have the  cash he doesn't have the political  capital look at the telegraph front page  and this story will come as depressing  reading to many families already reeling  from the national insurance increase  that they have to face but now according  to the telegraph uh council tax bills  will also uh increase uh come april  anita  yes i think this one is really i mean  it's a pretty speculative story but  absolutely given we know the way that  the council tax is structured um there's  likely to be some leeway for councils um  to raise the precept that people pay on  their council tax which could go towards  supporting again social care um and i  think that actually this is quite an  important story in the context of the  conservative party seeing some possible  movements in the polls as a result of it  in my view really gripping something  there's been a challenge for almost a  decade to ensure there's a better flow  of funding and a broader base of funding  for social care and i kind of think that  this is sort of what we want to see  which is investment both from  local councils but also from additional  taxation susie uh government spokesmans  are quoted as saying that we will ensure  every council has the resources they  need to deliver these reforms with extra  money invested next year and 5.4 billion  by 2024-25  yeah it's not a particularly speculative  story because it mentions here there is  a clause within the announcement last  week that says the cat the cap on local  authorities raising their council tax  precept for social care may well be  lifted in the budget next month  so it does look as though the tax rise  we were sold as being the once in a  lifetime sort of settlement for social  care  is not going to be anything of the sort  and we need to remember of course local  authorities bore the brunt of about 50  of the austerity cuts and they're the  ones who provide social care unless the  government hands money out it's not  gonna work we are running out of time  very quickly anita tell us about the  cows in the star  um  i think that what we're seeing is um a  possible new innovation it's incredibly  exciting what we might see  is cows being bred um to sort of stop  the ways in which their methane  emissions massively affect climate  change so we might be saving the world  one cow at a time very well done anita  you didn't say the dreaded word that i  think the star have used thank you both

